WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.066 --> 00:00:02.135
Spec-Driven Development moves the work from

2
00:00:02.135 --> 00:00:04.537
the how to the what and why.

3
00:00:04.838 --> 00:00:07.841
Since agents and models progress so fast,

4
00:00:08.041 --> 00:00:11.144
you don't want your workflow tied to just one choice.

5
00:00:11.478 --> 00:00:15.482
In this lesson, we see how standards let us switch agents

6
00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:19.285
while keeping our workflow and even our tools.

7
00:00:19.986 --> 00:00:21.988
These agent standards help for this goal.

8
00:00:22.389 --> 00:00:25.191
MCP for external tools,

9
00:00:25.191 --> 00:00:27.594
AGENTS.md for rules,

10
00:00:27.594 --> 00:00:31.164
Agent Skills for capturing repeatable workflows

11
00:00:31.164 --> 00:00:33.266
with extra context,

12
00:00:33.266 --> 00:00:37.170
and ACP for connecting agents to clients.

13
00:00:37.504 --> 00:00:41.608
For example, Codex is a leading AI agent from OpenAI.

14
00:00:42.108 --> 00:00:44.511
It runs in a desktop app and

15
00:00:44.511 --> 00:00:47.213
in editors as well as in a terminal.

16
00:00:47.814 --> 00:00:49.816
Let's see it in action.

17
00:00:50.517 --> 00:00:54.521
Here is our feature spec skill from lesson 12 copied into Codex,

18
00:00:54.621 --> 00:00:57.524
which stores them in a different path.

19
00:00:58.825 --> 00:01:01.027
It runs just fine once migrated.

20
00:01:01.528 --> 00:01:03.296
This lets you switch back and

21
00:01:03.296 --> 00:01:05.398
forth between agents in the same project,

22
00:01:05.999 --> 00:01:08.201
keeping your SDD workflow.

23
00:01:09.002 --> 00:01:13.006
We can also use different editors with different agents

24
00:01:13.106 --> 00:01:16.109
using the Agent Client Protocol standard.

25
00:01:16.709 --> 00:01:19.712
ACP makes it easy to connect agents and editors.

26
00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:22.682
If your agent and client support ACP,

27
00:01:22.782 --> 00:01:24.784
you have a perfect match.

28
00:01:25.285 --> 00:01:27.987
To make this plug and play even easier,

29
00:01:28.088 --> 00:01:30.957
the ACP registry automates finding,

30
00:01:31.057 --> 00:01:34.561
installing, and connecting agents with their clients.

31
00:01:34.894 --> 00:01:37.397
The ACP Registry covers the whole life cycle,

32
00:01:37.697 --> 00:01:40.100
making it easier to mix and match.

33
00:01:41.401 --> 00:01:43.703
For example, in JetBrains IDEs,

34
00:01:44.104 --> 00:01:48.675
the AI Chat window leads to installation from the ACP Registry.

35
00:01:49.342 --> 00:01:52.846
The IDE can then show a listing of compatible agents.

36
00:01:54.347 --> 00:01:56.850
OpenCode is a popular open source agent.

37
00:01:57.550 --> 00:02:01.554
The ACP registry makes it easy to add to our IDE.

38
00:02:02.255 --> 00:02:06.860
Clicking install automates both the installation of OpenCode itself if needed,

39
00:02:07.127 --> 00:02:10.330
which is nice, and integration into the IDE.

40
00:02:10.997 --> 00:02:15.997
Once finished, your IDE now has native integration with a new agent.

41
00:02:17.604 --> 00:02:20.974
You can use this new agent alongside the other agents.

42
00:02:21.441 --> 00:02:25.245
in your same editor as part of SDD.

43
00:02:26.246 --> 00:02:29.649
These new agent standards are bringing new possibilities,

44
00:02:29.949 --> 00:02:31.951
but how do they actually work?

45
00:02:32.418 --> 00:02:35.221
The ACP architecture was designed to ease

46
00:02:35.321 --> 00:02:37.724
plugging together agents and clients.

47
00:02:38.424 --> 00:02:41.828
In fact, the protocol matches what's used in LSP.

48
00:02:42.629 --> 00:02:44.731
ACP is more than you think.

49
00:02:44.831 --> 00:02:47.433
For example, it covers Next Edit Suggestion

50
00:02:47.534 --> 00:02:50.436
in the editor and plan mode.

51
00:02:50.904 --> 00:02:53.106
To go one step further with ACP,

52
00:02:53.106 --> 00:02:55.675
you can write your own custom agent,

53
00:02:55.675 --> 00:02:58.478
install it locally in your tool.

54
00:02:59.078 --> 00:03:02.081
Which agent to choose? The industry changes fast

55
00:03:02.081 --> 00:03:04.384
and benchmark sites have sprung up

56
00:03:04.384 --> 00:03:08.087
with leaderboards providing different evaluations.

57
00:03:08.688 --> 00:03:11.090
Of course, these leaderboards change fast.

58
00:03:11.491 --> 00:03:13.693
So make sure to keep up to date

59
00:03:13.693 --> 00:03:16.663
and base your decision on the criteria that matter to you.

60
00:03:16.663 --> 00:03:18.965
Our specs work at a higher level,

61
00:03:19.265 --> 00:03:21.367
not tied to any one agent or IDE.

62
00:03:21.668 --> 00:03:23.670
In this lesson, we prove that.

63
00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:26.072
We built our workflow and tools

64
00:03:26.272 --> 00:03:28.274
to be independent of the agent.

65
00:03:28.575 --> 00:03:31.544
As more standards emerge, this flexibility should grow.
